   <text>
      <front>
         <docTitle>
            <titlePart type="MainTitle">
               <emph rend="bold">A Heracleopolite Sale of Barley on Delivery</emph>
            </titlePart>
         </docTitle>
      </front>
      <body>
         <div type="article">
            <div type="epidoc" subtype="DDB">
               <div type="introduction">
                  <p xml:id="p1">The document was first published as a ‘Quittung über ein Getreidedarlehen’ (<ref target="https://papyri.info/biblio/43715">Gerstinger 1961</ref>: 59–60), later republished as <ref target="http://papyri.info/hgv/22902">SB 8 9773</ref> under the title ‘Quittungshomologie über den Empfang eines Getreidedarlehens’. Its correct identification as a ‘Kauf bei Vorauszahlung’ was first recognized by P.J. Sijpesteijn, who also offered a number of corrections to the text (<ref target="https://papyri.info/biblio/52194">Sijpesteijn 1977</ref>: 98 n. 20, 108–109). Shortly thereafter, R.S. Bagnall and K.A. Worp made further improvements, establishing the date of the text as 540 (<ref target="https://papyri.info/biblio/52330">Bagnall and Worp 1978</ref>: 227–229), previously placed in 405. These corrections were not incorporated into the <emph rend="italics">Berichtigungsliste</emph>, since a new edition in <emph rend="italics">Sammelbuch</emph> had been envisaged (<ref target="https://beehive.zaw.uni-heidelberg.de/info/45113">BL 7.214</ref>). No such edition has appeared, however, and the text as it currently stands cannot be used without consulting these two articles. While revising the text for DDbDP, it became clear that not all earlier proposals could be confirmed, that further emendations were required, and that, following autopsy, more than two dozen adjustments to brackets and underdots had to be made. Although the number of genuinely new readings is limited, the need for a reliable and readily accessible text justifies the present edition.</p>
                  <p xml:id="p2">The document records that a priest of a church of Heracleopolis acknowledges receipt from Aurelia Manna of the full price of ten artabas of barley, paid in advance of delivery. The name of Manna’s father was intended to be added later but was never supplied. Six years earlier, in 534, Aurelia Manna, daughter of Ioannes and Ania, and Aurelia Nymphe, daughter of Apollos and Thamoule, appear as lessors of four arouras of land to the bishop of Heracleopolis, with a deacon of the cathedral church acting as guarantor for the women (<ref target="https://papyri.info/hgv/22923">SB 8 9876</ref>). It is likely that both texts concern the same Manna; taken together, they form a small dossier documenting the financial dealings of a propertied woman who repeatedly engaged with representatives of the local church. The circumstances underlying these transactions cannot be determined on present evidence.</p>
                  <figure>
                     <ptr ana="hc:HeidICONImageResourceReference" target="https://heidicon.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/detail/24253866"/>
                     <head>Fig. 1: P.Vindob. Inv. G 26248 recto (= SB 8 9773r). © Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, Papyrussammlung.</head>
                  </figure>
                  <figure>
                     <ptr ana="hc:HeidICONImageResourceReference" target="https://heidicon.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/detail/24253870"/>
                     <head>Fig. 1: P.Vindob. Inv. G 26248 verso (= SB 8 9773v). © Österreichische Nationalbibliothek, Papyrussammlung.</head>
                  </figure>
                  <table type="papyrological_header">
                     <row>
                        <cell style="text-align: left;">P.Vindob. G 26248</cell>
                        <cell style="text-align: center;">10 (h) × 17 (w) cm</cell>
                        <cell style="text-align: right;">28 Sept. – 5 Oct. 540</cell>
                     </row>
                     <row>
                        <cell style="text-align: left;"/>
                        <cell style="text-align: left;"/>
                        <cell style="text-align: right;">Heracleopolis</cell>
                     </row>
                  </table>
               </div>
               <div copyOf="#ed1" type="edition"/>
               <div copyOf="#trans1" type="translation"/>
               <div type="commentary">
                  <note target="#ed1r1ln1">
                     <ref>1–6</ref>
                     <p xml:id="p3"> The reading of these lines is essentially after <ref target="https://papyri.info/biblio/52330">Bagnall and Worp 1978</ref>: 228. We have only transposed some dots and brackets.</p>
                  </note>
                  <note target="#ed1r1ln5">
                     <ref>5</ref>
                     <p xml:id="p4"> Φαωφ[ι   ̣  ̣  ̣  ̣]ῃ̣: Φαωφ[ι εἰκάδ]ι <ref target="https://papyri.info/biblio/52330">Bagnall and Worp 1978</ref>: 228. [πρώτ]ῃ̣, [τρίτ]ῃ̣, [ἕκτ]ῃ̣, [ὀγδό]ῃ̣ (28, 30 September; 3, 5 October) would fit.</p>
                  </note>
                  <note target="#ed1r1ln8">
                     <ref>8</ref>
                     <p xml:id="p5"> Ἀπανα̣κίο̣υ is after <ref target="https://papyri.info/biblio/52194">Sijpesteijn 1977</ref>: 108.</p>
                  </note>
                  <note target="#ed1r1ln10">
                     <ref>10–11</ref>
                     <p xml:id="p6"> Cf. <ref target="https://papyri.info/hgv/22923">SB 8 9876</ref>.5–6 (534) Αὐρη]λίαι Μάννα θυγάτηρ Ἰωάννου, μητρὸς | Ἀνίας καὶ Ν[ύμφη θυγάτ]ηρ Ἀπολλῶ, μητρὸς Θαμούλης.</p>
                  </note>
                  <note target="#ed1r1ln11">
                     <ref>11</ref>
                     <p xml:id="p7"> The <emph rend="italics">vacat</emph> was first recognized by <ref target="https://papyri.info/biblio/52194">Sijpesteijn 1977</ref>: 109.</p>
                     <p xml:id="p8">Ἡρακλέ̣[ου]ς ̣ πόλεως: Ἡρακλ[εου]πόλεως ed. pr.</p>
                  </note>
                  <note target="#ed1r1ln15">
                     <ref>15</ref>
                     <p xml:id="p9"> ⟨ἀρταβῶν⟩ δέκα μέ̣τ̣ρῳ̣ is due to <ref target="https://papyri.info/biblio/52194">Sijpesteijn 1977</ref>: 109.</p>
                     <p xml:id="p10">τούτων: τούτου ed. pr.</p>
                  </note>
                  <note target="#ed1r1ln18">
                     <ref>18</ref>
                     <p xml:id="p11"> καρπῶν. The first edition restored γεννημάτων, which is unparalleled and too long for the available space. The phrase ἀπὸ τῶν καρπῶν is not attested in this position in any of the few Heracleopolite documents of this type (<ref target="https://papyri.info/hgv/36582">SPP 20 148</ref>.15 has only καρπῶν), though it is known from other regions, including the neighbouring Fayum; cf. e.g. <ref target="https://papyri.info/hgv/41065">CPR 10 23</ref>.7 (Ars.; 523). </p>
                  </note>
                  <note target="#ed1r1ln19">
                     <ref>19–20</ref>
                     <p xml:id="p12"> [ἀνυπερθέτως καὶ] ἄ̣ν̣ευ πά[ση]ς | [ἀντιλογίας. τὸ γραμ]μ̣άτιον [κύριον]. <ref target="https://papyri.info/biblio/52194">Sijpesteijn 1977</ref>: 108 proposed [ἀνυπερθέτως καὶ ἄν]ευ πά[σης ἀντιλογίας] | [τῆς πράξεώς σοι οὔσης] κ̣α̣ὶ̣ τ̣ο̣[ῖς παρὰ σοῦ] (he numbered these lines as 18–19) There is no room for ἀντιλογίας in l. 19, and it is unlikely that the word was divided between the lines. Furthermore, there is a tiny horizontal stroke immediately after the lacuna, which we take to belong to the extended cap of the final sigma of πά[ση]ς ̣. In l. 20, κ̣α̣ὶ̣ τ̣ο̣[ῖς is not a possible reading; ed. pr. read το]ῦ̣ ἁγίου, which is close to what the papyrus has but not correct. We read γραμ]μ̣άτιον, as expected at this point in Heracleopolite documents; cf. e.g. <ref target="https://papyri.info/hgv/36582">SPP 20 148</ref>.19 (6th c.). This formulation is another indication that <ref target="https://papyri.info/hgv/851609">P.Hoogendijk 39</ref>, endorsed Ἡ]ρ̣ακλ(  ), is Heracleopolite.</p>
                  </note>
                  <note target="#ed1r1ln21">
                     <ref>21</ref>
                     <p xml:id="p13"> [καὶ ἐπερ(ωτηθεὶς) ὡμολ(όγησα). Ἰο]ύ̣λ̣ι̣ο̣[ς]. The first edition read [δι’ ἐμοῦ ]ου ἐ̣[τελειώθη], while <ref target="https://papyri.info/biblio/52194">Sijpesteijn 1977</ref>: 108 stated that the traces were ‘zu undeutlich, um eine Deutung zu wagen’.</p>
                  </note>
                  <head>Fr. 2</head>
                  <note target="ed1r2ln1">
                     <ref>1</ref>
                     <p xml:id="p14"> A composite of two minute, possibly contiguous fragments mounted in the same frame as fr. 1 and originally glued together 
upside down, has since been separated. ]κει in l. 1 could be part of προκείμενος, which would have belonged to Ioulios’ declaration and l. 21, that is, [πρεσβύτερος υἱὸς Ἀπανακίου ὁ προ]κεί|[μενος], but the remains of the next two lines cannot be placed in this context. An alternative is ὡς πρό]κει|[ται, from the end of the declaration, but it is again impossible to reconstruct a text that could include the rest of the fragment. </p>
                  </note>
                  <head>Back</head>
                  <note target="ed1vln1">
                     <ref>1</ref>
                     <p xml:id="p15"> χι(ρόγραφον): ὁμο]λ(ογία) ed. pr. The monogram chi-iota for χειρόγραφον is standard in endorsements of Arsinoite and Heracleopolite documents in this period; see <ref target="https://papyri.info/biblio/97711">Monte (2024)</ref>, with a list of attestations in Table 1.1, pp. 197–200.</p>
                     <p xml:id="p16">Ἀπα̣ν̣ακίο(υ): cf. <ref target="https://papyri.info/biblio/52194">Sijpesteijn 1977</ref>: 109.</p>
                     <p xml:id="p17">ἀπ̣[ὸ] κώμ̣η̣[ς Πεενη] <ref target="https://papyri.info/biblio/52194">Sijpesteijn 1977</ref>: 98 n. 20, who further notes that the name of the village may have been followed by a reference to the nome.</p>
                  </note>
               </div>
            </div>
